A||||27||NASAReeves||Fiero Audio...I need your advice, and some bass||||5||
Z||000000||NASAReeves||05-23-2001||06:54 PM||||I've read several posts lately regarding Fiero Audio.  I recently installed a new Kenwood Receiver (KDC-V6017), along with a nice pair of Kenwood 4x10 (Front) & 4x6 (Rear) speakers.  I don't have an amplifier installed, but this radio is pretty robust (47Wx4).  Everything on my '88GT is stock, except the radio & speakers.<P>The results are heavenly sound.  I have excellent clarity at all volumes, and I haven't even cranked it to the edge yet.  One problem...my system could use a touch of bass.<P>My question is, money aside, what is the easiest way to add bass to my Fiero.  My '88GT doesn't have a factory subwoofer...heck...I don't even know where the factory subwoofer goes.  As you all know, space is limited.<P>Can anyone make some suggestions, without calling for a permanent or unsightly modification, to add some bass?  What are some of the tricks to this trade?<P>Thanks!!<BR>Scott<BR><P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||sqoach||05-23-2001||06:58 PM||||Woohoo! I get to reply first!<P>Let's see...unsightly and easily removed. Look at this   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/footwellsub_1_2.JPG">  <P>And I know stimp and some other people will reply. Some members have put an 8" sub in the stock location. And even a 6 1/2" sub.<P>By the way, the stock location is right above that footpad on the passenger side. There's a big hole there.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">    <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||05-23-2001||07:09 PM||||I have an 8" stuffed in the footwell that is in a tuned sealed box. It takes up a little less room than the 10" boxes, But it sounds like a 10" is in the car. I'm only running 170 watts into it and the total cost for all (amp, sub, and wood for custom box) was under $200.00. For those that don't believe an 8" can sound like a 10" just ask Haze what he thought.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>You can get a 6 1/2" sub in the stock location, (right side of dash just above the foot pad) But an 8" is not a good idea. The limited space will not properly support that size woofer.<BR>Look in the mall section AusFiero is making a replacement console that holds a sub in it.<P>You locations are limited but creative. Just do a search for "Subs" and you'll see the many locations.<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">     <BR><B><I>Got Emblems?</I></B><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||fyerstarter||05-23-2001||07:31 PM||||Well here's your options as far as I know:<P>-Factory sub, 6", out of sight out of mind, pretty expensive and hard to find<P>-Make your own "factory sub". Buy a 6.5" sub (JL just came out with some I've been dying to try) and make your own "box" from pvc pipe or something. Mount in factory location.<P>-Center console box (replaces the glovebox), can get for 10 or 12" woofers. Available from AusFiero, WestCoast Fiero, RSM Motorsports or make your own.<P>-Footwell box: Stimpy sells em, can hold an 8 or 10, can make your own, takes up some room in your passenger compartment. <P>-Truck style boxes or bazooka tubes behind the seats. <P>-Bandpass box under the hood. Seen this done a few times. Basically a box with 2 10's or 12's under the hood with ports running into the passenger compartment. <P>As for which is best I'm not sure. But I plan on making my own footwell enclosure soon.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||MoneypitGT||05-23-2001||07:38 PM||||Put a 12 up front and port it.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.customdynamix.com/images/box.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.customdynamix.com/images/radio.jpg">  Damn my car is dirty. Time to clean it I think.<BR>  Chris<P>------------------<BR><B>Check out my site at:</B>   <A HREF="http://www.customdynamix.com" TARGET=_blank>www.customdynamix.com</A> <BR>2 '87 GT Projects<p>[This message has been edited by MoneypitGT (edited 05-23-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Formula88||05-23-2001||11:26 PM||||Yeah, I'm looking for some ideas, too, but I don't want a footwell box cuz my lady has long legs and likes to stretch out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> So, it's either in the stock location, or something behind the seat.  I'll also check out the console boxes.||||reg||10
Z||000006||stimpy||05-24-2001||01:03 AM||||Well, my PR machine has been working in high gear. Thanks guys. Here's what we're talking about...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=258191&a=1921599&p=34099626&Sequence=5&res=high"> <BR>As far as legroom goes, I'm 6'6", and I'm a LOT more comfortable with the footwell box then  would be with the seat slid forward the 3-4" required for a behind the seat box or the 8" a Bazooka style sub would require, That's just me though.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||05-24-2001||01:23 AM||||I tried the sub box behind the seat but it looks lame with the seat up all the way like that.  I should have my sub box in soon so I will find out how much better the footwell idea is.  But with the pass. seat all the way like it is now, I can barely reach the face of where the sub box is going to be anyway.  Most girls I know aren't over 5'5", I am 5'11" and I drive with my seat almost all the way back, so with the other seat all the way up its just weird.  Not to mention the back massage gets annoying as hell.<p>[This message has been edited by Black88GT (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||05-24-2001||07:03 AM||||Hey i forgot the website but someone put 4  12" subs behind the seat (I think they were 12"s anyway)   does anyone have the URL?  I was just thinking about this when a friend took him 94 S-10 Standard cab to a Audio install shop and had 3 JL's installed behind the seat, he hits 152 DB in side... thats alittle to much bass but still.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||08:52 AM||||I totally disagree that an 8" in stock location is a bad idea. I built a FG box that uses that space and protrudes about 2" downward. It has near 0.33cf. Installed an 8" RF XLC and the sound is the same or better as when I had it in a sealed box in the footwell. It gets 120W from 5th channel Kenwood. An 8" RF or Solobaric requires only about 0.3cf on sealed box. I have a drawing of the box's dimensions if anyone is interested in posting it (firewall doesn't let me). Soon will have pictures too.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||05-24-2001||09:59 AM||||Alex,<BR>When you have a few minutes, I'd like to see your plans and pics too, whenever you have them.<BR>I have been thinking about using an 8" driver(s) as well, possibly in an isobaric configuration. I haven't made any measurements, so I don't know if that will save me any space or not.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 black Formula<BR>88 Duke coupe <BR>88 "wadded up" Formula (parts car)||||reg||1
Z||000011||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||10:36 AM||||Raydar;  Sent you the mold dimensions diagram. Pictures should be ready in 2 weeks (take photos, processing, etc.).||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||05-24-2001||10:46 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Alex4mula:<BR><B>I totally disagree that an 8" in stock location is a bad idea. I built a FG box that uses that space and protrudes about 2" downward. It has near 0.33cf. Installed an 8" RF XLC and the sound is the same or better as when I had it in a sealed box in the footwell. It gets 120W from 5th channel Kenwood. An 8" RF or Solobaric requires only about 0.3cf on sealed box. I have a drawing of the box's dimensions if anyone is interested in posting it (firewall doesn't let me). Soon will have pictures too.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The max size in the space is .33 with your 2" extension. Yes the RF speaker requires .30cf of space. Now take the .33cf and subtract the displacement of the speaker which happens to be .12. You are now at .21.<BR>That means you are 1/3rd of a size smaller than required. (That's an awful lot of space on a small box!) This cuts down the lower frequencies of the sub. (Tightens up the bass) This means the bass will hit hard but not sustain the tone. So it will not sound "full". If the tightened and supressed sound is what you like, that is fine. But I prefer the the clarity, definition, and accuracy out of my XLC 8" sub.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||11:08 AM||||Oreif; My box volume is 0.37 (Raydar has diagram, maybe he can post) Minus sub displacement (.08) we have 0.29 or 3.3% smaller than spec (not 16.6% as you calculated). Sorry but I don't think that small difference will have a noticeable audible impact (human detectable). I have tested it with Bass-Mechanik CD tracks and it moves ok to the high 30s as it is spec.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Oreif||05-24-2001||11:25 AM||||How do yo get .37? When I measured mine the most I could get was .35 internal volume using 1/4" fiberglass. If you go to Rockfords Website and type in .37cf, once you install the XLC speaker it reads back as .23. (The spec sheet shows a displacement of .12 so I don't know why their volume calculator shows more) The box I'm using now is .45 which gets me to .33.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Jefrysuko||05-24-2001||11:50 AM||||Have any of you considered putting some type of tube or the like in front of the seats?  In my first fiero I had two kicker substations which was a long rectangular box with a 6" in it.  They were just barley over 6" high and wide and a little over a foot long.  They actually act as an extended leg rest which was very comftorable on long trips.  Short people got a kick out of riding in the passenger seat and dangleing their feet over them.  The only problem they caused was that you could no longer move the seat back and forth if you mounted them down.  It didn't bother me at all.  I am 6'4" keep the seat all the way back and don't really want anyone else driving my cars.  On the up side though there was a great place to mount them, the front seat bolts, and the wires hide very nicely.<P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>88 GT<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Carrolles||05-24-2001||11:59 AM||||Alex,<P>I'd like the info on your enclosure also.<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll<BR>carrolles@aol.com||||reg||1
Z||000017||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||12:23 PM||||Oreif; You don't know how I get 0.37 because you have not seen my box diagram. The box sticks out at the bottom providing more volume (space). Now you edited all the specs I was using. I guess I'll better get them myself. I'm glad you have the right box volume. Anyway I still disagree with you and anybody that the sound difference will be easily noticeable using this box size. Still if you want you can make it bigger to get the exact dimension spec required.||||reg||1
Z||000018||sqoach||05-24-2001||12:34 PM||||Actually, one of the best installs I've seen is Fierosound's. It's cool. And doesn't take up any space. (well, some, but the seats can still go back all the way (I think))<BR>One day, sqoach will have an install like that  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000019||Johnson's85SE||05-24-2001||12:55 PM||||Hi,<BR> I installed two tens one behind each seat with out cutting the firewall. I got great tight base. I loved it. But because of the length of the subs, ultimate SB10's the box had to be 3-5 inches from the firewall. the seats where pushed up one notch from the front one. I drove around like this 4spd std for a year, and now that the sterio is out I still have to have my seat like that, or I am uncomfortable. If I do reinstall, possibly selling car, I will definitly cut threw the firewall to place the magnets in. less base more room. Cheers! <BR><P>------------------<BR>If it isn't broke, it's not my car.||||reg||1
Z||000020||perks88gt||05-24-2001||02:10 PM||||I got to go with the guy who had the 6/12 in front of the seats.  If your looking for the "cheap", non-altering method, you should go for the new SAS Bazoooka 6/12 tubes, 150rms watts.  Im over 6'0, and cant afford to loose any space in the fiero.  The stock location idea is good if you whant to custom make your own box, but again for simplicity and money reasons wich the original post was asking for, go for the tubes.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||03:04 PM||||I agree with Perks. Custom boxes are far from simple. A Bazooka would be better for that.||||reg||1
Z||000022||NASAReeves||05-24-2001||04:00 PM||||Yeah, guys...I'm not into heavy modification these days.  I just don't have the time.<P>I appreciate everyone's advice.  Here's what I decided to do:  Kenwood has a new product KSC-WA62RC (a self-contained, amplified, sub-in-a-box kit).  Crutchfield is the only place that I have found that can sell it...and they don't even have their first shipment in.  I'm on their waiting list now.  LOL...I can't even find the dimensions of the unit.  <P>Oh well, I'll review the product & keep you all informed of how it works out when I receive it.  <P>BTW...I KNOW you guys know the answer to this--What's the smoothest way/path to run the ground & power to the sub's amp from the rear battery?  I'm thinking about just grounding off of the chassis, but I still need some advice on the hot side of things.  <P>THANKS!!!<P>BTW...what do you think? <A HREF="http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-qtIYQsBWGVE/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=51000&I=113WA62RC&o=&a=" TARGET=_blank>http://www.crutchfield.com/cgi-bin/S-qtIYQsBWGVE/ProdView.asp?s=0&c=3&g=51000&I =113WA62RC&o=&a=</A> <P><BR>  &/or  <A HREF="http://www.kenwoodusa.com/cat.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.kenwoodusa.com/cat.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR>Scott & Traci<BR>'88 GT<P>[This message has been edited by NASAReeves (edited 05-24-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by NASAReeves (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000023||Alex4mula||05-24-2001||04:09 PM||||Hay NASA, That looks nice. And good price too. Did they give you the dimensions? Their web doesn't show any details.||||reg||1
